        "content": "\n          <p><strong>Quick-Take Standard:</strong> Roaching occurs when someone hides multiple ongoing romantic or sexual partners while leading each person to believe they are in an exclusive courtship. In university environments, this manifests as hidden sneaky links and secret rosters, necessitating strict sexual health awareness, transparency demands, and swift boundary enforcement.</p>\n          <p>The term \"roaching\" originates from the old pest control adage: when you see one cockroach in the light, there are twenty more hiding in the dark. In university dating, roaching describes the deceptive practice of leading a student to believe that they are in an exclusive, budding romance, while secretly managing an entire roster of hidden partners behind the scenes.</p>\n          <p>On African campuses, roaching is closely intertwined with the culture of the \"sneaky link\"—secret romantic or sexual partners who are accessed only under cover of darkness in off-campus lodges, kept strictly compartmentalized from social circles, and hidden from public view. While multi-dating during early talking stages is normal when transparent, roaching relies on active deception, false promises of exclusivity, and calculated secrecy.</p>\n          <p>This covert behavior carries serious dangers for university students. Beyond the profound emotional betrayal of discovering you are just one of five secret partners, roaching poses catastrophic risks to reproductive health, exposing unsuspecting students to sexually transmitted infections (STIs) through non-consensual multi-partner networks. Unmasking roaching is essential for personal self-defense.</p>\n          <p>In close-knit student communities, the reputational fallout from roaching is devastating. When secret rosters are exposed—often during hostel arguments or anonymous campus confession channels—the resulting scandal disrupts academic focus and damages social standing across faculties.</p>\n        "
